  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA trough running along the Canadian border will send a Canadian \nsurface front through the forecast area and will bring a northerly\nwind shift and a brief cooldown tonight into Monday. Moisture \nwithin the westerly flow aloft will maintain variable middle and \nhigher level cloudiness and perhaps a few spotty light showers. \nRidging aloft strengthens and brings a return to hot conditions \nheading towards the middle of the week. Emebedded shortwaves along\nthe ridge are expected to interact with the monsoonal moisture \nleading to daily chances for showers and thunderstorms across the \nregion, especially in Southwest Montana. The main concern, \nespecially for Wednesday, will be an elevated chance for heavy \nrainfall which may lead to localized flash flooding.\n\nTowards the weekend, the ridge axis moves eastward allowing for \ndrier air to filter in and temperatures to warm across the region.\nThere are hints in the models that this ridge could break down \ntowards the weekend which would help moderate some of the \ncurrently forecasted highs but it would come at the price of \nwindier conditions which may still lead to increase fire weather \nconcerns. -thor\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorm Potential Wednesday thru Friday:\n\nAfternoon showers and thunderstorms are expected both on \nWednesday and Thursday. The severe potential looks a bit marginal \nat the moment and will largely hinge on the timing of the trough. \nThere is a concern that PWATs are running a bit high (>1.25\" \nacross much of the region) which has been further confirmed by the\nstrong signal on the NAEFS. The main impact from this would be \nlocalized flash flooding as well as the standard thunderstorm \nthreats of lightning, hail, and strong winds.\n\nTowards Friday, the threat for thunderstorms continues but the \nmoisture content goes down quite a bit compared to Wednesday and \nThursday. This opens the door for more of a threat for drier \nthunderstorms which, combined with hotter temperatures and lower \nminimum RH values, may increase the fire weather concerns for that\nafternoon. -thor\n\nGusty Winds and Elevated Fire Weather Conditions on Friday and \nSaturday...\n\nCross barrier flow will begin to increase through the end of the \nwork week and into the weekend as upper level ridging flattens, \nwith H700 winds increasing to between 25-35kts which is 1 to \nnearly 2 standard deviations above normal for the Rocky Mountain \nFront. NBM5.0 probabilities support a 35-70% chance for wind gusts\nto exceed 34kts on both days, with a 10-30% chance to exceed \n43kts. These gusty winds combined with hot temperatures will lead \nto elevated fire weather conditions along the entire Rocky \nMountain Front, most notably across Fire Weather Zones 112. \n- Moldan",
